mailing list I maintain is

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.760
one of the largest security mailing lists on the net.

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:34.040
There's probably a few thousand subscribers to it.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.780
We have a policy of full disclosure,

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:40.420
which means when a new security vulnerability is discovered,

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.460
we usually publish full details about it.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.950
And the reason we do this is because normally when an organization such as SIRT,

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:52.710
which is federally funded to do such a thing as published adversaries

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:53.650
and security vulnerabilities,

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:58.090
they do it a very long time after they've been told about the security problem.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:12:59.810
so for example

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.860
Somebody would go up to SIRT and tell them, we found a bug.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:06.200
SIRT will talk to the vendors, and they'll learn those who know about it,

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.700
but they'll take several months to publish a solution to the problem.

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:12.400
And by this time, the probability is that hackers know about it,

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.020
and they're out there breaking into machines.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:18.080
So BugTrack is kind of almost pushing the vendors to fix those problems faster?

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.360
Exactly, by putting fear on their heart to make them publish.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:23.280
But then the hackers are going to BugTrack, too.

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:25.500
So, I mean, they're finding out some of those problems

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.600
and really even pushing the security break.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.400
Yes, I mean, it is a trade-off.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.880
You trade off a bit of that security to have independents, you know, come up with patches as fast as possible.

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:41.960
